WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.679
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar and you're watching Mind Your Decisions.

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:08.429
A square with a side length of five is

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:16.108
positioned with its corner in the center of a square with a side length of four as shown in the diagram.

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:23.759
One side of the overlapping region between the two squares is equal to three.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:29.848
The puzzle is to calculate the area of the overlap between the two squares.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.840
That is, to calculate the area of the red shape.

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:39.869
I thank Ohad from Israel for suggesting this problem. Can you figure it out?

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:45.419
Give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching the video for a solution.

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:02.579
So, how can we figure this problem out?

00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:09.929
Well, the first thing is the side of three is actually irrelevant to this problem. We'll remove it.

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:16.619
Next we'll draw blue lines to extend the sides of the square as shown.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.460
What does this do?

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:26.759
Notice the square with a side length of four is now divided into four identical shapes,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.029
each with an area equal to the red shape.

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:35.860
Therefore,

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.509
each shape is equal to one-fourth of the squares area.

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:43.978
So the red region has an area equal to the area of the square,

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:48.898
which is 4 times 4, or 4 squared,
and then we divide it by 4

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:51.509
This will be equal to 4.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.960
Amazing!

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:03.340
In fact, we can extend this problem that the area of overlap between the two
squares always has an area of 4

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:09.580
So we consider a square with a side length of 4 and then we have a square with a side length of 5.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:17.990
What we'll do is we'll create a tiling where we have four different squares with a side length of five.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:27.770
Notice the square with a side of four is
divided into four identical shapes right
now, each with an area equal to four.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:34.369
Now this will be true even as we rotate the squares with a side length of five.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:41.629
We still have the square with the side length of four being divided into four identical shapes each with an area equal to four.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:47.239
Now we can continue the symmetry as we rotate the larger squares.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:55.420
The square with a side length of four is always divided into four different identical shapes.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.780
Therefore each will have an area that's one-fourth the area of the square.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.540
It's a pretty neat problem,

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.200
and rotational symmetry solves this like magic.

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:08.959
Did you figure it out?

